  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 1255 AM CDT Fri Jul 24 2026\n\nSunday through Thursday:\n\nThere are better chances for showers and thunderstorms at times\n(30 to 40 percent) Sunday afternoon into Monday evening, as \nweak warm air advection lingers with possible 500 mb vorticity \nmaxima shifting through the area at times. In addition, the low \nlevel jet nose may develop rounds of convection over southern \nMinnesota and northern Iowa and shift it southeastward into or \nclipping parts of southern Wisconsin. \n\nSunday/Sunday night may be tougher to get storms to develop or \nmaintain themselves than Monday/Monday night, as the capping \nwill remain fairly strong per area forecast soundings. Again, it\nmay be more likely that something moves southeast into the \narea, though it will be battling a more capped environment. \n\nThe capping on Monday afternoon and night is far lower, which \nwould help convection develop and maintain strength as it shifts\nsoutheast through the area. Models are also hinting at a low \npressure system shifting southeast through the western Great \nLakes region either later Monday or Monday night, which may drag\na cold front through and help with upward vertical motion. \n\nFor the Sunday afternoon into Monday evening period, there will\nbe a lot of mean layer CAPE (over 3000 J/kg) with increasing \ndeep layer bulk shear (25 knots Sunday/Sunday night and 25 to 30\nknots or more Monday/Monday night). So, severe storms are \npossible if something can get going. The uncertainty lies with \ntiming and where storms may track, and if there will be several \nrounds. Keep up with the forecast for this period.\n\nIn addition, highs should reach into the lower 90s Sunday and\nMonday, with dew points into the lower to middle 70s. This may\nbring heat index values to around or a little above 100 degrees,\nso heat headlines may eventually be needed if this comes to\nfruition. Warm and humid conditions are likely to linger through\nnext week per ensemble trends and the low temperature spreads \non the NBM.\n\nThere may be a lull in the active weather Tuesday into\nWednesday, as high pressure moves through in continued northwest\nflow aloft. There may be more active weather again later in the\nweek, as more systems start to shift southeastward through the\nregion at times per ensemble member trends. \n\nWood",
